<div class="pn" id="p-about" hidden><div class="ph"><span>About Bitruvius</span><span><button class="pm">–</button> <button class="px">×</button></span></div><div class="pb">
<p><i>“This is Bitruvius. He was born here, raised here. Some would argue that he’s trapped here, but that’s up to you. Bitruvius is a puppet. An actor. A character. You can click on any bone you see and replace it with your own asset. He lives in a 2D world, but sometimes there seems to be more than one dimension at play.”</i></p>
<details open><summary>The paper puppet</summary><p>Bitruvius Puppyt is a paper puppet (papyr puppyt) powered by computational precision, for the creators working between 2D and 3D, in the tradition of Lotte Reiniger and hinged silhouette animation. Every piece serves two purposes:</p><ul><li>an immediate anatomical building block for an artist putting ink to physical paper</li><li>an easily swappable visual asset for digital cutout animators and storytellers</li></ul><p>Vast computational power packed inside a tiny, lightweight puppet.</p></details>
<details><summary>A computer, not a creator</summary><p>AI is a computer, and it must be used as a computer. No pretending to be an artist, no word salad, no intellectual casino. Like a classic sci-fi terminal, it does precise vectors, exact mathematics, code parsing and spatial transformations, in a basic, open and replicable way, and it adds nothing the human did not mean.</p></details>
<details open><summary>Principles</summary><ul><li><b>Zero generative slop.</b> No prompt boxes, synthetic images or diffusion. Pure deterministic rigging.</li><li><b>Dual-purpose building block.</b> A high-contrast silhouette: ink-on-paper reference and swappable 2.5D cutout puppet.</li><li><b>Deterministic stage math.</b> A 2,500 × 8,742 pyxl canvas, centerline MX = 1,257 pyxl, head ruler 1H = 1,240 pyxl (toggle <i>stage math</i> to see it).</li><li><b>1-click / 2-click export.</b> One click saves a pose PNG; two clicks (<i>+ Key</i>) keyframe pose-to-pose cutout animation.</li><li><b>Open and granular.</b> One readable file, no black boxes.</li></ul></details>
<p>The face is a hierarchy: skull → forehead, crown, cheeks, eye frames (eyes, sockets), nose, upper lip (teeth) and the chin/mandible (jowls, lower teeth, chinpin). Open the head rig to see it as an indented tree. Five parts hinge on one origin, the centre of the upper lip (◉): the chin, both cheeks and both upper jowls. The lower jowls form the corners of the open mouth and hang from the chin. The origin sits on the face centreline, so the bones stay put and <i>flip face</i> switches sides for a side pose.</p><p class="dim">Hips pivot at the bulb of each femur. The back-wing joint (faint, digital mode) swings the blade/paddle about the neck for side views; the swivel tip beyond each shoulder aims the swivel. Click a bone or piece and drag to rotate. Alt-drag is IK. Double-click the head, a hand or a foot to open its rig (Yorrick, Gotz, Piggy). The whole figure is framed automatically (ankle one foot length above the bottom, golden point 0.6 up) until you zoom or pan; Fit or F frames it again. Shift-drag pans, wheel zooms, Space plays, K keys a frame. Double right-click saves JSON, triple right-click saves a PNG. Hover a joint and click it; click a finger, thumb or toe tip and drag to aim that bone. Panels can be dragged out, collapsed and stacked; double-click a panel title to dock it again.</p></div></div>
